          guideText: `{name}. Last lesson we crossed into Eastern philosophy and found deep wisdom asking somewhat different questions than the Western tradition. Today we follow that thread to traditions that are, for many families, much closer to home — including, perhaps, your own. We're going to explore Indigenous ways of knowing, and the Latin American healing traditions known as curanderismo: bodies of knowledge and philosophy rooted not in libraries and laboratories, but in relationship, in land, in community, and in practice handed down across generations. And I want to set this up with real care, the same care we brought to Eastern philosophy, because the stakes of getting it right are high. For a long time, much of this knowledge was dismissed by outsiders as "primitive" or "superstition" — a dismissal that was both arrogant and, it turns out, frequently wrong: a great deal of Indigenous ecological and plant knowledge has since been confirmed by science, and some of it taught science things it didn't know. So our first job is respect: to take these as serious ways of understanding the world, on their own terms. But respect is not the same as switching off your judgment. The traditions themselves are not monoliths, they update over time, and — just like Eastern wisdom — there is a whole industry of outsiders selling distorted, commodified versions: "buy your spiritual healing here," fake "shamans," exploitation dressed up as reverence. So we hold both, as always: deep respect AND clear eyes, with one more note today — humility about what is not ours to claim, sell, or pretend to master. Let's explore knowledge that lives in relationship.`,
          headline: `Indigenous & Curandero Ways of Knowing`,
          subtitle: `Knowledge rooted in relationship, land, and community — honored on its own terms, with respect and clear eyes. A matching exploration.`,
