          headline: `Respect, Romanticizing, And The Counterfeit Trade`,
          paragraphs: [
            `Now the protective heart, which has a special shape for these traditions. With Eastern philosophy, the danger was mostly people selling distorted versions of someone else's wisdom. Here that danger exists too — and it has an added, sharper edge, because these traditions belong to peoples who were colonized, whose knowledge was first stolen or suppressed and is now, in a cruel twist, often repackaged and sold back by outsiders. So there's a real and booming counterfeit trade: "plastic shamans" with no actual training or community standing, charging tourists thousands for "authentic ancient ceremonies"; mass-produced "smudge kits" and "spirit animal" merchandise stripped of any real meaning; expensive "healing retreats" that borrow the prestige and aesthetics of real traditions while having little connection to them. This isn't just inaccurate, the way a bad science claim is inaccurate; it's often exploitation — profiting off the image of peoples while disrespecting the actual living communities and, frequently, the people themselves.`,
            `This gives a clear thinker an extra tool here, beyond the usual tests: pay attention to relationship, community, and humility, because the real traditions are saturated with these and the counterfeits almost never are. Genuine traditional knowledge lives inside a community, is held with responsibility, is usually not for sale to anyone with a credit card, and tends to come with humility and obligation rather than grand promises. The counterfeit, by contrast, is typically detached from any real community, sold to strangers as a product, promises quick transformation or secret power, and is marketed by someone with no accountable relationship to the tradition they're invoking. When you see "ancient sacred healing" being sold as a slick consumer experience to outsiders, with big promises and a big price and no real community behind it, that pattern itself is the warning sign. And notice the irony: the counterfeit usually romanticizes the tradition (exotic, mystical, magical) precisely in order to sell it — so the gullible romantic and the profiteer are often working hand in hand, both substituting a flattering fantasy for the real, grounded, demanding thing.`,
            `So the mature stance pulls together everything, with one addition special to this lesson: humility about what is not yours. Take these knowledge-systems seriously as real philosophy and real knowledge — relational, place-based, communal, holistic — and recognize how much that was dismissed has proven true. Keep your clear thinking switched on: respect is not credulity, traditions contain a mix like all human knowledge, and serious medical conditions need real medical care. Refuse both the colonizer's arrogant dismissal and the tourist's gullible romanticization, since both fail to engage the knowledge as knowledge. And add this: approach traditions that are not your own with humility — as something to learn from and respect, not to claim mastery of, costume yourself in, or sell. If a tradition IS part of your heritage, you can hold it with love and pride AND with honest, clear eyes; that is not betrayal, it is the deepest kind of respect, the kind that engages the real thing rather than a museum piece or a marketing image. Open heart, clear eyes, and humble hands.`,
          ],
